/* NCERT Physics Class XI — Chapter 1: Units and Measurement (book page 1–12)
   type 'num' = Numerical (step-by-step solution in sol); img = inline SVG graph/diagram */
addChapter({
  id: 'phy-11-01',
  subj: 'phy',
  cls: 11,
  no: 1,
  title: 'Units and Measurement',
  src: 'NCERT Physics XI · Chapter 1',
  points: [
    { id: 'in1', topic: '1.1 Introduction', t: 'Measurement of any physical quantity involves comparison with a certain basic, arbitrarily chosen, internationally accepted reference standard called unit. The result of a measurement is expressed by a number (numerical measure) accompanied by a unit.' },
    { id: 'in2', topic: 'Base and derived units', t: 'Although the number of physical quantities is very large, only a limited number of units is needed since they are inter-related. Units for the fundamental or base quantities are called fundamental or base units; units of all other quantities, expressed as combinations of base units, are called derived units. A complete set of base and derived units is known as the system of units.' },
    { id: 'si1', topic: '1.2 CGS, FPS, MKS', t: 'Earlier, three systems were used extensively: CGS (centimetre, gram, second), FPS or British (foot, pound, second) and MKS (metre, kilogram, second) for length, mass and time respectively.' },
    { id: 'si2', topic: 'SI', t: 'The system at present internationally accepted is the Système International d\' Unités (French for International System of Units), abbreviated SI. It was developed by the Bureau International des poids et mesures (BIPM) in 1971 and recently revised by the General Conference on Weights and Measures in November 2018. Because SI uses a decimal system, conversions within the system are simple and convenient.' },
    { id: 'si3', topic: 'Seven base units', t: 'In SI there are seven base units (Table 1.1): length – metre (m), mass – kilogram (kg), time – second (s), electric current – ampere (A), thermodynamic temperature – kelvin (K), amount of substance – mole (mol), luminous intensity – candela (cd).' },
    { id: 'si4', topic: 'Radian and steradian', t: 'Two more units are defined: (a) plane angle dθ = ratio of length of arc ds to radius r, unit radian (rad); (b) solid angle dΩ = ratio of intercepted area dA of the spherical surface, described about the apex O as centre, to the square of its radius r, unit steradian (sr). Both are dimensionless quantities (Fig 1.1).' },
    { id: 'f11', topic: 'Fig 1.1', t: 'Fig 1.1 (a) plane angle dθ = ds/r radian, with arc ds at distance r from O; (b) solid angle dΩ = dA/r² steradian, with area dA on a sphere of radius r about apex O.' },
    { id: 't11a', topic: 'Table 1.1 definitions (1)', t: 'Table 1.1: metre is defined by fixing the speed of light in vacuum c = 299792458 m s⁻¹; kilogram by fixing Planck constant h = 6.62607015×10⁻³⁴ J s (kg m² s⁻¹); second by fixing the caesium frequency ΔνCs, the unperturbed ground-state hyperfine transition frequency of caesium-133 atom = 9192631770 Hz (s⁻¹).' },
    { id: 't11b', topic: 'Table 1.1 definitions (2)', t: 'Table 1.1: ampere by fixing elementary charge e = 1.602176634×10⁻¹⁹ C (A s); kelvin by fixing Boltzmann constant k = 1.380649×10⁻²³ J K⁻¹; mole contains exactly 6.02214076×10²³ elementary entities (Avogadro constant NA in mol⁻¹); candela by fixing luminous efficacy Kcd of monochromatic radiation of frequency 540×10¹² Hz to be 683 lm W⁻¹. These values need not be remembered; definitions are revised to keep up with progress in measurement.' },
    { id: 'mol', topic: 'Mole entities', t: 'When mole is used, the elementary entities must be specified. These may be atoms, molecules, ions, electrons, other particles or specified groups of such particles.' },
    { id: 't12', topic: 'Table 1.2', t: 'Table 1.2 units retained for general use (outside SI): minute 60 s; hour 3600 s; day 86400 s; year 365.25 d = 3.156×10⁷ s; degree 1° = (π/180) rad; litre 1 dm³ = 10⁻³ m³; tonne 10³ kg; carat 200 mg; bar 0.1 MPa = 10⁵ Pa; curie 3.7×10¹⁰ s⁻¹; roentgen 2.58×10⁻⁴ C/kg; quintal 100 kg; barn 100 fm² = 10⁻²⁸ m²; are 1 dam² = 10² m²; hectare 1 hm² = 10⁴ m²; standard atmospheric pressure 1 atm = 101325 Pa = 1.013×10⁵ Pa.' },
    { id: 'app', topic: 'Appendices', t: 'Some derived units in terms of SI base units are given in Appendix A 6.1, derived units with special names in A 6.2 and A 6.3. Common SI prefixes for multiples and sub-multiples are given in Appendix A2; guidelines for symbols in A7 and A8.' },
    { id: 'sf1', topic: '1.3 Significant figures', t: 'Every measurement involves errors. The reported result includes all digits known reliably plus the first digit that is uncertain; these are significant digits or significant figures. E.g. period 1.62 s: 1 and 6 reliable, 2 uncertain — three significant figures. Length 287.5 cm has four significant figures. Reporting more digits than significant is superfluous and misleading.' },
    { id: 'sf2', topic: 'Precision and change of units', t: 'Significant figures indicate the precision of measurement, which depends on the least count of the measuring instrument. A choice of change of different units does not change the number of significant digits: 2.308 cm = 0.02308 m = 23.08 mm = 23080 μm, all with four significant figures. The location of the decimal point is of no consequence.' },
    { id: 'sr1', topic: 'Rules (1)', t: 'All the non-zero digits are significant. All the zeros between two non-zero digits are significant, no matter where the decimal point is. If the number is less than 1, the zero(s) on the right of decimal point but to the left of the first non-zero digit are not significant (in 0.002308 the underlined zeroes are not significant).' },
    { id: 'sr2', topic: 'Rules (2) trailing zeros', t: 'The terminal or trailing zero(s) in a number without a decimal point are not significant (123 m = 12300 cm = 123000 mm has three significant figures). The trailing zero(s) in a number with a decimal point are significant (3.500 and 0.06900 have four significant figures each).' },
    { id: 'sr3', topic: 'Confusion of trailing zeros', t: 'A length reported as 4.700 m has zeroes conveying precision, so they are significant. Changing units, 4.700 m = 470.0 cm = 4700 mm = 0.004700 km; one might wrongly conclude 4700 mm has two significant figures, but a mere change of units cannot change the number of significant figures.' },
    { id: 'sn1', topic: 'Scientific notation', t: 'To remove ambiguities, report every measurement in scientific notation: a × 10ᵇ, with 1 ≤ a < 10 and b any positive or negative power of 10. 4.700 m = 4.700 × 10² cm = 4.700 × 10³ mm = 4.700 × 10⁻³ km. The power of 10 is irrelevant to significant figures; all zeroes in the base number a are significant.' },
    { id: 'om1', topic: 'Order of magnitude', t: 'Rounding a to 1 (for a ≤ 5) and to 10 (for 5 < a ≤ 10), the number is approximately 10ᵇ; the exponent b is the order of magnitude. Diameter of earth 1.28×10⁷ m has order 7; diameter of hydrogen atom 1.06×10⁻¹⁰ m has order −10. So the earth\'s diameter is 17 orders of magnitude larger than the hydrogen atom.' },
    { id: 'sr4', topic: 'Rules (3)–(6)', t: 'Without scientific notation: for a number greater than 1 without any decimal, trailing zero(s) are not significant; for a number with a decimal, trailing zero(s) are significant. The 0 conventionally put to the left of a decimal for a number less than 1 (like 0.1250) is never significant, but zeroes at the end of such a number are significant.' },
    { id: 'ex0', topic: 'Exact numbers', t: 'Multiplying or dividing factors which are neither rounded numbers nor measured values are exact and have infinite number of significant digits: in r = d/2 or s = 2πr the factor 2 is exact (2.0, 2.00 or 2.0000 as required); in T = t/n, n is exact.' },
    { id: 'ar1', topic: '1.3.1 Arithmetic — multiplication/division', t: 'The result of a calculation cannot be more accurate than the original measured values. In multiplication or division, the final result should retain as many significant figures as there are in the original number with the least significant figures. Mass 4.237 g and volume 2.51 cm³ give density 1.69 g cm⁻³ (three significant figures), not 1.68804780876.' },
    { id: 'ar2', topic: 'Light year example', t: 'If speed of light is 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ (three significant figures) and one year (365.25 d) = 3.1557 × 10⁷ s (five), the light year is 9.47 × 10¹⁵ m (three significant figures).' },
    { id: 'ar3', topic: 'Addition/subtraction', t: 'In addition or subtraction, the final result should retain as many decimal places as there are in the number with the least decimal places. 436.32 g + 227.2 g + 0.301 g = 663.821 g → 663.8 g. Similarly 0.307 m − 0.304 m = 0.003 m = 3 × 10⁻³ m. Writing 664 g or 3.00 × 10⁻³ m would not convey the precision properly.' },
    { id: 'ro1', topic: '1.3.2 Rounding off', t: 'A number with more than one uncertain digit should be rounded off. 2.746 rounded to three significant figures is 2.75; 1.743 becomes 1.74. Rule: the preceding digit is raised by 1 if the insignificant digit to be dropped is more than 5, and left unchanged if it is less than 5.' },
    { id: 'ro2', topic: 'Dropping a 5', t: 'If the insignificant digit is 5: if the preceding digit is even, the 5 is simply dropped; if it is odd, the preceding digit is raised by 1. So 2.745 → 2.74 and 2.735 → 2.74 (three significant figures).' },
    { id: 'ro3', topic: 'Intermediate steps', t: 'In multi-step calculations, retain in intermediate steps one digit more than the significant digits and round off at the end. 2.99792458 × 10⁸ m/s is often rounded to 3 × 10⁸ m/s. Exact numbers such as 2π in T = 2π√(L/g) have a large (infinite) number of significant figures; π = 3.1415926... may be taken as 3.142 or 3.14 as required.' },
    { id: 'e11', topic: 'Example 1.1', t: 'Example 1.1: side of a cube 7.203 m (four significant figures). Surface area = 6(7.203)² = 311.299254 m² → 311.3 m²; volume = (7.203)³ = 373.714754 m³ → 373.7 m³.' },
    { id: 'e12', topic: 'Example 1.2', t: 'Example 1.2: 5.74 g of a substance occupies 1.2 cm³. Mass has 3 and volume only 2 significant figures, so density = 5.74/1.2 = 4.8 g cm⁻³ (2 significant figures).' },
    { id: 'un1', topic: '1.3.3 Uncertainty in products', t: 'Length and breadth of a sheet measured with a metre scale are 16.2 cm and 10.1 cm. l = 16.2 ± 0.1 cm = 16.2 cm ± 0.6 %; b = 10.1 ± 0.1 cm = 10.1 cm ± 1 %. Using the combination of errors rule, lb = 163.62 cm² ± 1.6 % = 163.62 ± 2.6 cm², quoted as lb = 164 ± 3 cm²; 3 cm² is the uncertainty in the area.' },
    { id: 'un2', topic: 'n significant figures; subtraction', t: 'If a set of experimental data is specified to n significant figures, a result obtained by combining the data will also be valid to n significant figures. However, subtraction can reduce the number: 12.9 g − 7.06 g is evaluated as 5.8 g (not 5.84 g), as uncertainties in addition/subtraction combine by decimal places.' },
    { id: 'un3', topic: 'Relative error', t: 'The relative error of a value specified to significant figures depends not only on n but also on the number itself. Mass 1.02 g ± 0.01 g has relative error (0.01/1.02) × 100 % = ±1 %; mass 9.89 g ± 0.01 g has relative error ±0.1 %.' },
    { id: 'un4', topic: 'One extra digit', t: 'Intermediate results in a multi-step computation should be calculated to one more significant figure than in the least precise measurement. The reciprocal of 9.58 rounded to three significant figures is 0.104, but the reciprocal of 0.104 is 9.62; writing 1/9.58 = 0.1044 and taking its reciprocal retrieves 9.58.' },
    { id: 'di1', topic: '1.4 Dimensions', t: 'All derived quantities can be expressed in terms of seven base quantities, called the seven dimensions: length [L], mass [M], time [T], electric current [A], thermodynamic temperature [K], luminous intensity [cd] and amount of substance [mol]. The dimensions of a physical quantity are the powers (or exponents) to which the base quantities are raised to represent that quantity.' },
    { id: 'di2', topic: 'Volume and force', t: 'Volume = length × breadth × height, dimensions [L]³ — zero dimension in mass [M⁰] and time [T⁰]. Force = mass × acceleration = mass × length/(time)², dimensions [M L T⁻²]: one dimension in mass, one in length and −2 in time.' },
    { id: 'di3', topic: 'Magnitudes not considered', t: 'In this representation magnitudes are not considered; it is the quality of the type of quantity. A change in velocity, initial velocity, average velocity, final velocity and speed are all equivalent, with dimensions [L T⁻¹].' },
    { id: 'df1', topic: '1.5 Dimensional formula', t: 'The expression showing how and which base quantities represent the dimensions of a physical quantity is its dimensional formula: volume [M⁰ L³ T⁰], speed [M⁰ L T⁻¹], acceleration [M⁰ L T⁻²], mass density [M L⁻³ T⁰].' },
    { id: 'df2', topic: 'Dimensional equation', t: 'An equation obtained by equating a physical quantity with its dimensional formula is its dimensional equation: [V] = [M⁰ L³ T⁰], [v] = [M⁰ L T⁻¹], [F] = [M L T⁻²], [ρ] = [M L⁻³ T⁰]. Dimensional formulae of many quantities are given in Appendix 9.' },
    { id: 'da1', topic: '1.6 Dimensional analysis', t: 'Only quantities having the same dimensions can be added or subtracted. Dimensional analysis helps in deducing relations among physical quantities and checking derivation, accuracy and dimensional consistency (homogeneity) of expressions. Units and dimensions are treated like algebraic symbols; identical ones in numerator and denominator cancel. Both sides of an equation must have the same dimensions.' },
    { id: 'dc1', topic: '1.6.1 Principle of homogeneity', t: 'Magnitudes can be added or subtracted only if they have the same dimensions — velocity cannot be added to force; current cannot be subtracted from temperature. This is the principle of homogeneity of dimensions. If dimensions of all terms are not the same, the equation is wrong.' },
    { id: 'dc2', topic: 'Limitations; dimensionless', t: 'Dimensional consistency does not guarantee correct equations; it is uncertain to the extent of dimensionless quantities or functions. Arguments of special functions (trigonometric, logarithmic, exponential) must be dimensionless. A pure number, ratio of similar quantities such as angle (length/length) and refractive index (speed of light in vacuum/speed in medium) has no dimensions.' },
    { id: 'dc3', topic: 'x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²', t: 'For x = x₀ + v₀t + (1/2)at²: [x] = [L], [x₀] = [L], [v₀t] = [L T⁻¹][T] = [L], [(1/2)at²] = [L T⁻²][T²] = [L]. Each term has dimension of length, so the equation is dimensionally correct.' },
    { id: 'dc4', topic: 'Test of units', t: 'A test of consistency of dimensions tells no more and no less than a test of consistency of units, but has the advantage that we need not commit to a particular choice of units or conversions. If an equation fails this test it is proved wrong; if it passes, it is not proved right. A dimensionally correct equation need not be exact, but a dimensionally wrong (inconsistent) equation must be wrong.' },
    { id: 'e13', topic: 'Example 1.3', t: 'Example 1.3: for ½mv² = mgh, LHS = [M][L T⁻¹]² = [M L² T⁻²] and RHS = [M][L T⁻²][L] = [M L² T⁻²]; the equation is dimensionally correct.' },
    { id: 'e14', topic: 'Example 1.4', t: 'Example 1.4: energy unit J = kg m² s⁻². For K = m²v³ dimensions [M² L³ T⁻³]; K = (1/2)mv² and K = (3/16)mv² give [M L² T⁻²]; K = ma gives [M L T⁻²]; K = (1/2)mv² + ma has no proper dimensions. So (a), (c), (e) are ruled out; dimensional arguments cannot tell which of (b) or (d) is correct — (b) is correct by definition.' },
    { id: 'dr1', topic: '1.6.2 Deducing relations', t: 'The method of dimensions can deduce a relation if we know the dependence of the quantity on others (up to three physical quantities or linearly independent variables), written as a product type of dependence.' },
    { id: 'e15', topic: 'Example 1.5', t: 'Example 1.5: time period T of a simple pendulum depending on length l, mass m and g: T = k lˣ gʸ mᶻ. [L⁰M⁰T¹] = [L]ˣ[L T⁻²]ʸ[M]ᶻ gives x + y = 0, −2y = 1, z = 0, so x = ½, y = −½, z = 0 and T = k√(l/g). Actually k = 2π, T = 2π√(l/g).' },
    { id: 'dr2', topic: 'Limits of the method', t: 'Dimensionless constants cannot be obtained by the method of dimensions. The method can only test dimensional validity, not the exact relationship, and does not distinguish between quantities having same dimensions.' },
    { id: 'su1', topic: 'Summary 1–6', t: 'Physics is a quantitative science based on measurement. Base quantities (length, mass, time, electric current, thermodynamic temperature, amount of substance, luminous intensity) have standardised units; other units are derived. SI based on seven base units is internationally accepted; units like joule, newton, watt are special names; standard symbols such as m, kg, s, A, N are used.' },
    { id: 'su2', topic: 'Summary 7–11', t: 'Small and large quantities are expressed in scientific notation with powers of 10 and prefixes. In computing, units of derived quantities are treated as algebraic quantities. Only proper significant figures are retained, with rules for arithmetic and rounding off. Dimensional analysis checks consistency and deduces relations; a dimensionally consistent equation need not be correct, but an inconsistent one must be wrong.' },
    { id: 'xe1', topic: 'Exercises 1.1–1.3', t: 'Exercises: 1 cm³ = 10⁻⁶ m³; 18 km h⁻¹ = 5 m s⁻¹; relative density of lead 11.3 → 11.3 g cm⁻³ = 1.13×10⁴ kg m⁻³; 1 kg m² s⁻² = 10⁷ g cm² s⁻²; 3.0 m s⁻² = 3.9×10⁴ km h⁻²; G = 6.67×10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² = 6.67×10⁻⁸ cm³ s⁻² g⁻¹; 1 calorie = 4.2 α⁻¹β⁻²γ² in units of mass α kg, length β m, time γ s.' },
    { id: 'xe2', topic: 'Exercises 1.4–1.9', t: '\'Large\' or \'small\' is meaningless without a standard for comparison. With speed of light as unit of speed, the Sun–Earth distance is 500 light-seconds (light takes 8 min 20 s). An optical instrument measuring to within a wavelength of light is the most precise length device. Hair seen at 3.5 mm under magnification 100 is 0.035 mm thick. A slide area 1.75 cm² projected to 1.55 m² gives linear magnification √(1.55×10⁴/1.75) ≈ 94.1.' },
    { id: 'xe3', topic: 'Exercises 1.10–1.17', t: 'Exercises: significant figures and rounding (e.g. 0.007 m² has 1; 2.64×10²⁴ kg has 3; 0.2370 g cm⁻³ has 4; 6.320 J has 4; 6.032 N m⁻² has 4; 0.0006032 m² has 4); m = m₀/(1 − v²/c²)^½ (c placed by dimensions); 1 Å = 10⁻¹⁰ m; molar volume 22.4 L; nearby objects seem to move fast opposite to a train while distant ones seem stationary (parallax); Sun\'s density ≈ 1.4×10³ kg m⁻³, in the range of solids and liquids.' }
  ],
  qs: [
    { id: 'q1', p: ['in1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The basic, arbitrarily chosen, internationally accepted reference standard used for comparison in measurement is called:', o: ['Dimension', 'Unit', 'Significant figure', 'Order of magnitude'], a: 1, e: 'Result = number × unit.' },
    { id: 'q2', p: ['in1', 'in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The result of a measurement of a physical quantity is expressed by:', o: ['A unit only', 'A dimension only', 'A number accompanied by a unit', 'A number only'], a: 2, e: 'Numerical measure + unit.' },
    { id: 'q3', p: ['in2'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Units of base quantities are called fundamental or base units.', 'A complete set of base and derived units is known as the system of units.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true (1.1).' },
    { id: 'q4', p: ['in2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Units obtained for derived quantities as combinations of base units are called:', o: ['Fundamental units', 'Supplementary units', 'Practical units', 'Derived units'], a: 3, e: 'Derived units.' },
    { id: 'q5', p: ['si1'], type: 'match', lvl: 1, q: 'Match the system of units with its base units of length, mass and time.', match: { h: ['System', 'Base units'], l: ['CGS', 'FPS', 'MKS'], r: ['metre, kilogram, second', 'centimetre, gram, second', 'foot, pound, second'] }, o: ['A-I, B-II, C-III', 'A-II, B-III, C-I', 'A-III, B-I, C-II', 'A-II, B-I, C-III'], a: 1, e: 'FPS is the British system.' },
    { id: 'q6', p: ['si1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The FPS system is also known as:', o: ['British system', 'French system', 'Metric system', 'International system'], a: 0, e: '1.2.' },
    { id: 'q7', p: ['si2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The SI system was developed by BIPM in the year:', o: ['1960', '1971', '2018', '1889'], a: 1, e: 'Revised in November 2018.' },
    { id: 'q8', p: ['si2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['SI was recently revised by the General Conference on Weights and Measures in November 2018.', 'Conversions within SI are complicated because it is not a decimal system.'], a: 2, e: 'SI uses a decimal system, so conversions are simple.' },
    { id: 'q9', p: ['si2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Conversions within the SI system are quite complicated.', R: 'SI units use a decimal system.' }, a: 3, e: 'A is false: SI conversions are simple because SI uses a decimal system.' },
    { id: 'q10', p: ['si3'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following are SI base units?', s: ['kelvin', 'candela', 'newton', 'mole'], o: ['I, II and III only', 'I, II and IV only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'Newton is a derived unit.' },
    { id: 'q11', p: ['si3'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the base quantity with its SI unit symbol.', match: { h: ['Base quantity', 'Symbol'], l: ['Thermodynamic temperature', 'Luminous intensity', 'Amount of substance', 'Electric current'], r: ['A', 'mol', 'cd', 'K'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 2, e: 'Table 1.1.' },
    { id: 'q12', p: ['si3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'How many base units are there in SI?', o: ['Five', 'Six', 'Seven', 'Nine'], a: 2, e: 'Table 1.1.' },
    { id: 'q13', p: ['si4', 'f11'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The SI unit of solid angle is:', o: ['Radian', 'Degree', 'Square metre', 'Steradian'], a: 3, e: 'dΩ = dA/r² steradian.' },
    { id: 'q14', p: ['si4', 'f11'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 130" width="300" height="130" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20" y1="100" x2="140" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="20" y1="100" x2="125" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 140 100 A 120 120 0 0 0 125 40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M 45 100 A 25 25 0 0 0 42 88" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="15" y="104" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><text x="80" y="64" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">r</text><text x="145" y="70" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ds</text><text x="56" y="94" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dθ</text><text x="80" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(a)</text><line x1="175" y1="80" x2="280" y2="30" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="175" y1="80" x2="285" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 280 30 Q 300 70 285 110" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M 280 30 Q 268 70 285 110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="170" y="84" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><text x="225" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">r</text><text x="296" y="74" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dA</text><text x="205" y="84" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dΩ</text><text x="230" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(b)</text></svg>', q: 'Solid angle dΩ subtended at apex O by area dA of a sphere of radius r is:', o: ['dA/r²', 'dA/r', 'r²/dA', 'dA·r²'], a: 0, e: 'Fig 1.1(b).' },
    { id: 'q15', p: ['si4', 'f11'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, tag: '🖼 Diagram', img: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 300 130" width="300" height="130" stroke-linecap="round" role="img"><line x1="20" y1="100" x2="140" y2="100" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="20" y1="100" x2="125" y2="40" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 140 100 A 120 120 0 0 0 125 40" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M 45 100 A 25 25 0 0 0 42 88" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><text x="15" y="104" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><text x="80" y="64" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">r</text><text x="145" y="70" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">ds</text><text x="56" y="94" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dθ</text><text x="80" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(a)</text><line x1="175" y1="80" x2="280" y2="30" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><line x1="175" y1="80" x2="285" y2="110" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4"/><path d="M 280 30 Q 300 70 285 110" fill="none" stroke="#D9A520" stroke-width="2.4"/><path d="M 280 30 Q 268 70 285 110" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.4" stroke-dasharray="3 3"/><text x="170" y="84" text-anchor="end" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">O</text><text x="225" y="48" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">r</text><text x="296" y="74" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dA</text><text x="205" y="84" text-anchor="start" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor">dΩ</text><text x="230" y="122" text-anchor="middle" font-family="Inter,Arial,sans-serif" font-size="11" fill="currentColor" font-weight="700">(b)</text></svg>', q: 'An arc of length 3.0 cm subtends an angle at the centre of a circle of radius 6.0 cm. The angle is:', o: ['2.0 rad', '0.50 rad', '0.25 rad', '18 rad'], a: 1, e: 'dθ = ds/r.', sol: ['Given: ds = 3.0 cm, r = 6.0 cm', 'Formula: θ = ds/r', 'Substitution: θ = 3.0/6.0', 'Answer: θ = 0.50 rad'] },
    { id: 'q16', p: ['si4'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'An area of 0.20 m² on a sphere of radius 2.0 m subtends at its centre a solid angle of:', o: ['0.10 sr', '0.40 sr', '0.050 sr', '0.80 sr'], a: 2, e: 'dΩ = dA/r².', sol: ['Given: dA = 0.20 m², r = 2.0 m', 'Formula: Ω = dA/r²', 'Substitution: Ω = 0.20/(2.0)² = 0.20/4.0', 'Answer: Ω = 0.050 sr'] },
    { id: 'q17', p: ['si4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Plane angle is defined as the ratio of the radius to the arc length.', 'Radian and steradian are dimensionless quantities.'], a: 3, e: 'dθ = ds/r — arc length over radius.' },
    { id: 'q18', p: ['t11a'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The SI unit metre is defined by taking the fixed numerical value of:', o: ['Planck constant', 'Speed of light in vacuum', 'Boltzmann constant', 'Elementary charge'], a: 1, e: 'c = 299792458 m s⁻¹.' },
    { id: 'q19', p: ['t11a', 't11b'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the SI base unit with the constant fixed in its definition.', match: { h: ['Unit', 'Constant'], l: ['kilogram', 'ampere', 'kelvin', 'second'], r: ['Caesium frequency ΔνCs', 'Boltzmann constant k', 'Elementary charge e', 'Planck constant h'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-II, B-III, C-IV, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I'], a: 3, e: 'Table 1.1.' },
    { id: 'q20', p: ['t11a'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The second is defined using the hyperfine transition frequency of which atom?', o: ['Krypton-86', 'Carbon-12', 'Caesium-133', 'Hydrogen-1'], a: 2, e: '9192631770 Hz.' },
    { id: 'q21', p: ['t11b'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'One mole contains exactly how many elementary entities?', o: ['6.62607015 × 10⁻³⁴', '1.380649 × 10⁻²³', '1.602176634 × 10⁻¹⁹', '6.02214076 × 10²³'], a: 3, e: 'Avogadro number.' },
    { id: 'q22', p: ['t11b'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The candela is defined using monochromatic radiation of frequency 540 × 10¹² Hz.', 'The luminous efficacy Kcd of this radiation is fixed at 683 lm W⁻¹.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true (Table 1.1).' },
    { id: 'q23', p: ['mol'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Elementary entities may be atoms, molecules, ions or electrons.', 'When mole is used, the elementary entities need not be specified.'], a: 2, e: 'The entities must be specified.' },
    { id: 'q24', p: ['t12'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: '1 bar is equal to:', o: ['10⁵ Pa', '10³ Pa', '1.013 × 10⁵ Pa', '10⁶ Pa'], a: 0, e: 'Table 1.2: 0.1 MPa.' },
    { id: 'q25', p: ['t12'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the unit (outside SI) with its value.', match: { h: ['Unit', 'Value'], l: ['carat', 'quintal', 'barn', 'hectare'], r: ['10⁴ m²', '10⁻²⁸ m²', '200 mg', '100 kg'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-III, C-I, D-II', 'A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I', 'A-II, B-IV, C-III, D-I'], a: 0, e: 'Table 1.2.' },
    { id: 'q26', p: ['t12'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Using Table 1.2 (1 year = 3.156 × 10⁷ s), the number of seconds in 2 years is about:', o: ['3.156 × 10⁷ s', '1.728 × 10⁵ s', '6.312 × 10⁸ s', '6.312 × 10⁷ s'], a: 3, e: 'Multiply by 2.', sol: ['Given: 1 y = 3.156 × 10⁷ s', 'Formula: t = 2 × 1 y', 'Substitution: t = 2 × 3.156 × 10⁷', 'Answer: 6.312 × 10⁷ s'] },
    { id: 'q27', p: ['t12'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Standard atmospheric pressure in bar is approximately:', o: ['1.013 bar', '0.1013 bar', '10.13 bar', '101.3 bar'], a: 0, e: '1 bar = 10⁵ Pa.', sol: ['Given: 1 atm = 1.013 × 10⁵ Pa, 1 bar = 10⁵ Pa', 'Formula: atm in bar = (1.013 × 10⁵)/(10⁵)', 'Substitution: = 1.013', 'Answer: ≈ 1.013 bar'] },
    { id: 'q28', p: ['t12'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: '1 litre is equal to:', o: ['10⁻⁶ m³', '10⁻³ m³', '10³ m³', '1 m³'], a: 1, e: '1 L = 1 dm³.' },
    { id: 'q29', p: ['app'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Common SI prefixes for multiples and sub-multiples are listed in which appendix?', o: ['A6', 'A9', 'A2', 'A7'], a: 2, e: 'Appendix A2; dimensional formulae in Appendix 9.' },
    { id: 'q30', p: ['app', 'su1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Joule, newton and watt are examples of:', o: ['Base units', 'Units outside SI', 'Dimensionless units', 'Derived units with special names'], a: 3, e: 'Appendix A 6.2.' },
    { id: 'q31', p: ['sf1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Significant figures in a measured value are:', o: ['Reliable digits plus the first uncertain digit', 'Only the reliable digits', 'All digits including all uncertain ones', 'Only non-zero digits'], a: 0, e: '1.3.' },
    { id: 'q32', p: ['sf1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The period of oscillation of a pendulum is reported as 1.62 s. The number of significant figures and the uncertain digit are:', o: ['2; the digit 6', '3; the digit 2', '3; the digit 1', '2; the digit 2'], a: 1, e: '1 and 6 are reliable.', sol: ['Reliable digits: 1, 6', 'First uncertain digit: 2', 'Significant figures = reliable + first uncertain', 'Answer: 3 significant figures; 2 is uncertain'] },
    { id: 'q33', p: ['sf2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The length 2.308 cm, when written as 23.08 mm, still has four significant figures.', R: 'A change of units changes the number of significant figures.' }, a: 2, e: 'R is false: change of units does not change significant figures.' },
    { id: 'q34', p: ['sf2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The precision of a measurement depends on:', o: ['The unit chosen', 'The least count of the measuring instrument', 'The position of decimal point', 'The number of zeros written'], a: 1, e: '1.3.' },
    { id: 'q35', p: ['sr1', 'sr2', 'sr4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following numbers have exactly four significant figures?', s: ['0.06900', '3.500', '12300', '0.002308'], o: ['I and II only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: '12300 has three; others have four.' },
    { id: 'q36', p: ['sr1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The number of significant figures in 0.002308 is:', o: ['6', '7', '4', '3'], a: 2, e: 'Leading zeros are not significant.', sol: ['Number: 0.002308', 'Zeros before 2 (left of first non-zero digit): not significant', 'Significant digits: 2, 3, 0, 8 (zero between non-zero digits counts)', 'Answer: 4'] },
    { id: 'q37', p: ['sr2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The number of significant figures in 123000 mm (no decimal point) is:', o: ['6', '4', '5', '3'], a: 3, e: 'Trailing zeros without a decimal are not significant.', sol: ['Number: 123000 (no decimal point)', 'Rule: trailing zeros without decimal are not significant', 'Significant digits: 1, 2, 3', 'Answer: 3'] },
    { id: 'q38', p: ['sr2', 'sr4'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Trailing zeros in a number without a decimal point are significant.', 'Trailing zeros in a number with a decimal point are significant.'], a: 3, e: 'Without a decimal they are not significant.' },
    { id: 'q39', p: ['sr3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The value 4700 mm, obtained from 4.700 m, has four significant figures.', R: 'A mere change of units cannot change the number of significant figures.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q40', p: ['sr3', 'sn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'To remove ambiguity about trailing zeros in 4700 mm (from 4.700 m), it should be written as:', o: ['47 × 10² mm', '4.7 × 10³ mm', '4.700 × 10³ mm', '4700. mm × 10⁰'], a: 2, e: 'Scientific notation.' },
    { id: 'q41', p: ['sn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In scientific notation a × 10ᵇ, the number a lies in the range:', o: ['0 ≤ a < 1', '1 ≤ a < 100', '10 ≤ a < 100', '1 ≤ a < 10'], a: 3, e: '1.3 (3).' },
    { id: 'q42', p: ['sn1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In scientific notation, the power of 10 is irrelevant to the number of significant figures.', 'In scientific notation, zeros in the base number a are not significant.'], a: 2, e: 'All zeros in a are significant.' },
    { id: 'q43', p: ['om1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Diameter of the earth is 1.28 × 10⁷ m. Its order of magnitude is:', o: ['7', '8', '6', '1'], a: 0, e: 'a = 1.28 ≤ 5 rounds to 1.', sol: ['Given: 1.28 × 10⁷ m', 'Rule: a ≤ 5 → round a to 1', '1.28 × 10⁷ ≈ 1 × 10⁷', 'Answer: order of magnitude = 7'] },
    { id: 'q44', p: ['om1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Diameter of earth is 1.28 × 10⁷ m and of hydrogen atom 1.06 × 10⁻¹⁰ m. Earth\'s diameter is larger by how many orders of magnitude?', o: ['3', '17', '−3', '70'], a: 1, e: 'Orders 7 and −10.', sol: ['Order of earth\'s diameter = 7', 'Order of H-atom diameter = −10', 'Difference = 7 − (−10)', 'Answer: 17 orders of magnitude'] },
    { id: 'q45', p: ['om1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A quantity is 8.2 × 10⁻⁵ m. Its order of magnitude is:', o: ['−5', '−6', '−4', '5'], a: 2, e: 'a = 8.2 > 5 rounds to 10.', sol: ['Given: 8.2 × 10⁻⁵', 'Rule: 5 < a ≤ 10 → round a to 10', '8.2 × 10⁻⁵ ≈ 10 × 10⁻⁵ = 10⁻⁴', 'Answer: −4'] },
    { id: 'q46', p: ['sr4'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In 0.1250, the zero to the left of the decimal point is:', o: ['Never significant', 'Always significant', 'Significant only in SI', 'Significant if the number is > 1'], a: 0, e: 'Conventional zero.' },
    { id: 'q47', p: ['sr4', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Number of significant figures in 0.0006032 m² is:', o: ['7', '8', '3', '4'], a: 3, e: 'Leading zeros not significant.', sol: ['Number: 0.0006032', 'Leading zeros (0.000) are not significant', 'Significant digits: 6, 0, 3, 2', 'Answer: 4'] },
    { id: 'q48', p: ['ex0'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'In s = 2πr, the factor 2 has only one significant figure.', R: 'Exact numbers in formulae have an infinite number of significant digits.' }, a: 3, e: 'A is false; the factor 2 is exact.' },
    { id: 'q49', p: ['ex0'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'In T = t/n (time period from n oscillations), the number n is:', o: ['A measured value with one significant figure', 'An exact number with infinite significant digits', 'A rounded number', 'A dimensional constant'], a: 1, e: '1.3 (6).' },
    { id: 'q50', p: ['ar1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Mass of an object is 4.237 g and its volume is 2.51 cm³. Its density to correct significant figures is:', o: ['1.69 g cm⁻³', '1.688 g cm⁻³', '1.7 g cm⁻³', '1.68804 g cm⁻³'], a: 0, e: 'Least significant figures = 3.', sol: ['Given: m = 4.237 g (4 s.f.), V = 2.51 cm³ (3 s.f.)', 'Formula: ρ = m/V, keep 3 s.f.', 'Substitution: 4.237/2.51 = 1.688047...', 'Answer: 1.69 g cm⁻³'] },
    { id: 'q51', p: ['ar1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'In multiplication or division, the result should retain as many significant figures as the number with the least significant figures.', R: 'The result of a calculation cannot be more accurate than the original measured values.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q52', p: ['ar2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Speed of light 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and one year 3.1557 × 10⁷ s give one light year as:', o: ['9.4671 × 10¹⁵ m', '9.47 × 10¹⁵ m', '9.5 × 10¹⁵ m', '9 × 10¹⁵ m'], a: 1, e: 'Three significant figures.', sol: ['Given: c = 3.00 × 10⁸ (3 s.f.), t = 3.1557 × 10⁷ s (5 s.f.)', 'Formula: d = c × t, keep 3 s.f.', 'Substitution: 3.00 × 3.1557 × 10¹⁵ = 9.4671 × 10¹⁵', 'Answer: 9.47 × 10¹⁵ m'] },
    { id: 'q53', p: ['ar3'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The sum 436.32 g + 227.2 g + 0.301 g to correct significant figures is:', o: ['663.821 g', '664 g', '663.8 g', '663.82 g'], a: 2, e: 'Least decimal places = 1.', sol: ['Sum = 663.821 g', 'Least precise term 227.2 g has 1 decimal place', 'Round to 1 decimal place', 'Answer: 663.8 g'] },
    { id: 'q54', p: ['ar3'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: '0.307 m − 0.304 m expressed with correct precision is:', o: ['3.00 × 10⁻³ m', '0.0030 m', '3.0 × 10⁻³ m', '3 × 10⁻³ m'], a: 3, e: 'Decimal places rule.', sol: ['Difference = 0.003 m', 'Both have 3 decimal places', 'Result keeps 3 decimal places: 0.003 m', 'Answer: 3 × 10⁻³ m'] },
    { id: 'q55', p: ['ar3'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In subtraction, the multiplication rule of least significant figures is used.', 'In addition, the result keeps as many decimal places as the number with least decimal places.'], a: 3, e: 'Subtraction also follows decimal places.' },
    { id: 'q56', p: ['ar3', 'xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Gold pieces of masses 20.15 g and 20.17 g are weighed. The difference in their masses to correct significant figures is:', o: ['0.02 g', '0.020 g', '0.0200 g', '0.2 g'], a: 0, e: 'Decimal places rule.', sol: ['Given: 20.17 g and 20.15 g (2 decimal places each)', 'Difference = 20.17 − 20.15 = 0.02 g', 'Keep 2 decimal places', 'Answer: 0.02 g'] },
    { id: 'q57', p: ['ro1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: '2.746 rounded off to three significant figures is:', o: ['2.74', '2.75', '2.7', '2.746'], a: 1, e: 'Dropped digit 6 > 5.', sol: ['Number: 2.746', 'Digit to drop: 6 (> 5)', 'Raise preceding digit 4 → 5', 'Answer: 2.75'] },
    { id: 'q58', p: ['ro2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: '2.745 rounded off to three significant figures is:', o: ['2.75', '2.7', '2.74', '2.76'], a: 2, e: 'Preceding digit 4 is even.', sol: ['Number: 2.745, digit to drop = 5', 'Preceding digit 4 is even', 'Rule: drop the 5 simply', 'Answer: 2.74'] },
    { id: 'q59', p: ['ro2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: '2.735 rounded off to three significant figures is:', o: ['2.73', '2.7', '2.735', '2.74'], a: 3, e: 'Preceding digit 3 is odd.', sol: ['Number: 2.735, digit to drop = 5', 'Preceding digit 3 is odd', 'Rule: raise preceding digit by 1', 'Answer: 2.74'] },
    { id: 'q60', p: ['ro1', 'ro2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['1.743 rounded to three significant figures is 1.74.', '2.745 rounded to three significant figures is 2.75.'], a: 2, e: '2.745 → 2.74 (preceding digit even).' },
    { id: 'q61', p: ['ro3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'In a complex multi-step calculation, intermediate steps should retain:', o: ['All digits from the calculator', 'One digit less than the significant digits', 'One digit more than the significant digits', 'Only integers'], a: 2, e: 'Round off at the end.' },
    { id: 'q62', p: ['ro3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The factor 2π in T = 2π√(L/g) has:', o: ['Two significant figures', 'Three significant figures', 'No significant figures', 'A large (infinite) number of significant figures'], a: 3, e: 'Exact number.' },
    { id: 'q63', p: ['e11'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Each side of a cube is 7.203 m. Its total surface area to correct significant figures is:', o: ['311.3 m²', '311.299 m²', '311 m²', '51.88 m²'], a: 0, e: 'Four significant figures.', sol: ['Given: a = 7.203 m (4 s.f.)', 'Formula: S = 6a²', 'Substitution: 6 × (7.203)² = 311.299254', 'Answer: 311.3 m²'] },
    { id: 'q64', p: ['e11'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Each side of a cube is 7.203 m. Its volume to correct significant figures is:', o: ['373.71 m³', '373.7 m³', '374 m³', '373.714754 m³'], a: 1, e: 'Four significant figures.', sol: ['Given: a = 7.203 m', 'Formula: V = a³', 'Substitution: (7.203)³ = 373.714754', 'Answer: 373.7 m³'] },
    { id: 'q65', p: ['e12'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: '5.74 g of a substance occupies 1.2 cm³. Its density keeping significant figures in view is:', o: ['4.78 g cm⁻³', '4.783 g cm⁻³', '4.8 g cm⁻³', '5 g cm⁻³'], a: 2, e: 'Volume has only 2 significant figures.', sol: ['Given: m = 5.74 g (3 s.f.), V = 1.2 cm³ (2 s.f.)', 'Formula: ρ = m/V, keep 2 s.f.', 'Substitution: 5.74/1.2 = 4.783...', 'Answer: 4.8 g cm⁻³'] },
    { id: 'q66', p: ['un1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'l = 16.2 ± 0.1 cm and b = 10.1 ± 0.1 cm. The area of the sheet with its uncertainty is quoted as:', o: ['163.62 ± 0.2 cm²', '164 ± 0.2 cm²', '163.6 ± 1.6 cm²', '164 ± 3 cm²'], a: 3, e: 'Percentage errors add in a product.', sol: ['Relative errors: 0.1/16.2 ≈ 0.6 %, 0.1/10.1 ≈ 1 %', 'For product: total ≈ 0.6 % + 1 % = 1.6 %', 'lb = 163.62 cm², error = 1.6 % of 163.62 ≈ 2.6 cm²', 'Answer: lb = 164 ± 3 cm²'] },
    { id: 'q67', p: ['un1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A length is measured as 16.2 ± 0.1 cm. Its percentage error is about:', o: ['0.6 %', '1 %', '0.1 %', '6 %'], a: 0, e: 'Relative error × 100.', sol: ['Given: l = 16.2 cm, Δl = 0.1 cm', 'Formula: % error = (Δl/l) × 100', 'Substitution: (0.1/16.2) × 100 = 0.617', 'Answer: ≈ 0.6 %'] },
    { id: 'q68', p: ['un2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: '12.9 g − 7.06 g, both to three significant figures, is properly evaluated as:', o: ['5.84 g', '5.8 g', '5.840 g', '6 g'], a: 1, e: 'Subtraction can reduce significant figures.', sol: ['Difference = 5.84 g', 'Least decimal places: 12.9 g → 1 decimal', 'Round to 1 decimal place', 'Answer: 5.8 g'] },
    { id: 'q69', p: ['un2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Subtraction of data given to n significant figures can give a result with fewer significant figures.', R: 'In subtraction the result keeps as many significant figures as the number with the least significant figures.' }, a: 2, e: 'R is false: subtraction follows least decimal places.' },
    { id: 'q70', p: ['un3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Masses 1.02 g and 9.89 g are both measured to ± 0.01 g. Their relative errors are respectively:', o: ['± 0.1 % and ± 1 %', '± 1 % and ± 1 %', '± 1 % and ± 0.1 %', '± 0.01 % and ± 0.01 %'], a: 2, e: 'Relative error depends on the number itself.', sol: ['Relative error = (Δm/m) × 100', '1.02 g: (0.01/1.02) × 100 ≈ 1 %', '9.89 g: (0.01/9.89) × 100 ≈ 0.1 %', 'Answer: ± 1 % and ± 0.1 %'] },
    { id: 'q71', p: ['un3', 'un4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Relative error of a value depends on the number of significant figures as well as on the number itself.', 'Intermediate results should be calculated to one more significant figure than the least precise measurement.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q72', p: ['un4'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'The reciprocal of 9.58 is rounded to 0.104 (three significant figures). The reciprocal of 0.104 to three significant figures is:', o: ['9.58', '9.60', '9.6', '9.62'], a: 3, e: 'Rounding errors build up.', sol: ['1/9.58 = 0.10438... → 0.104', '1/0.104 = 9.615...', 'Round to three significant figures', 'Answer: 9.62 (original 9.58 is not retrieved)'] },
    { id: 'q73', p: ['di1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The dimensions of a physical quantity are:', o: ['The powers to which the base quantities are raised to represent it', 'The units of the quantity', 'The magnitude of the quantity', 'The number of significant figures'], a: 0, e: '1.4.' },
    { id: 'q74', p: ['di1'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following are among the seven dimensions of the physical world?', s: ['[L]', '[A]', '[cd]', '[F]'], o: ['I and II only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV', 'I, II and III only'], a: 3, e: 'Force is derived.' },
    { id: 'q75', p: ['di2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Force = mass × acceleration. The dimensions of force are:', o: ['[M L T⁻²]', '[M L² T⁻²]', '[M L T⁻¹]', '[M L⁻¹ T⁻²]'], a: 0, e: '1.4.', sol: ['Acceleration = length/(time)² → [L T⁻²]', 'Force = mass × acceleration', '[F] = [M] × [L T⁻²]', 'Answer: [M L T⁻²]'] },
    { id: 'q76', p: ['di2'], type: 'st', lvl: 1, st: ['Volume has zero dimension in mass and time.', 'Force has −2 dimensions in time.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q77', p: ['di3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Initial velocity, final velocity and speed all have the same dimensions.', R: 'Speed is a scalar while velocity is a vector.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true, but the reason for equal dimensions is that all are length/time.' },
    { id: 'q78', p: ['df1', 'df2'], type: 'match', lvl: 2, q: 'Match the quantity with its dimensional formula.', match: { h: ['Quantity', 'Dimensional formula'], l: ['Volume', 'Speed', 'Acceleration', 'Mass density'], r: ['[M L⁻³ T⁰]', '[M⁰ L T⁻²]', '[M⁰ L T⁻¹]', '[M⁰ L³ T⁰]'] }, o: ['A-III, B-IV, C-I, D-II', 'A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I', 'A-IV, B-II, C-III, D-I', 'A-I, B-III, C-II, D-IV'], a: 1, e: '1.5.' },
    { id: 'q79', p: ['df2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'An equation obtained by equating a physical quantity with its dimensional formula is called:', o: ['Dimensional formula', 'Dimensional equation', 'Equation of motion', 'Homogeneous equation'], a: 1, e: '1.5.' },
    { id: 'q80', p: ['df1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The dimensional formula of mass density is:', o: ['[M L³ T⁰]', '[M⁰ L⁻³ T⁰]', '[M L⁻³ T⁰]', '[M L⁻² T⁰]'], a: 2, e: 'Mass/volume.' },
    { id: 'q81', p: ['da1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Two physical quantities can be added or subtracted only if they have:', o: ['The same magnitude', 'Different dimensions', 'The same number of significant figures', 'The same dimensions'], a: 3, e: '1.6.' },
    { id: 'q82', p: ['dc1'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Velocity cannot be added to force.', R: 'Only quantities with the same dimensions can be added or subtracted.' }, a: 0, e: 'Principle of homogeneity.' },
    { id: 'q83', p: ['dc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The principle that all terms of a physical equation must have the same dimensions is called:', o: ['Principle of homogeneity of dimensions', 'Principle of superposition', 'Principle of least count', 'Principle of significant figures'], a: 0, e: '1.6.1.' },
    { id: 'q84', p: ['dc2'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following must be dimensionless?', s: ['Argument of sin θ', 'Argument of log x', 'Refractive index', 'Velocity'], o: ['I, II and III only', 'I and II only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 0, e: 'Velocity has dimensions [L T⁻¹].' },
    { id: 'q85', p: ['dc2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Dimensional consistency guarantees that an equation is correct.', 'Angle, being a ratio of lengths, has no dimensions.'], a: 3, e: 'It does not guarantee correctness.' },
    { id: 'q86', p: ['dc3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'In x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at², the dimensions of the term ½at² are:', o: ['[L T⁻²]', '[L]', '[L T]', '[L T⁻¹]'], a: 1, e: 'Dimensionally consistent.', sol: ['[a] = [L T⁻²], [t²] = [T²]', '½ is dimensionless', '[½at²] = [L T⁻²][T²]', 'Answer: [L]'] },
    { id: 'q87', p: ['dc4'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'A dimensionally wrong equation must be wrong.', R: 'A dimensionally correct equation need not be an exact equation.' }, a: 1, e: 'Both true; R does not explain A.' },
    { id: 'q88', p: ['dc4'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['A test of consistency of dimensions tells no more and no less than a test of consistency of units.', 'If an equation passes the dimensional test, it is proved right.'], a: 2, e: 'Passing does not prove it right.' },
    { id: 'q89', p: ['e13'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'For ½mv² = mgh, the dimensions of each side are:', o: ['[M L T⁻²]', '[M L² T⁻¹]', '[M L² T⁻²]', '[M² L² T⁻²]'], a: 2, e: 'Example 1.3.', sol: ['LHS: [M][L T⁻¹]² = [M L² T⁻²]', 'RHS: [M][L T⁻²][L] = [M L² T⁻²]', 'LHS = RHS', 'Answer: [M L² T⁻²]; equation is dimensionally correct'] },
    { id: 'q90', p: ['e14'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 3, q: 'Which formulae for kinetic energy K can be ruled out by dimensional arguments?', s: ['K = m²v³', 'K = ma', 'K = (1/2)mv² + ma', 'K = (3/16)mv²'], o: ['I and II only', 'I, II and III only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 1, e: 'K = (3/16)mv² is dimensionally correct (though not the actual formula).' },
    { id: 'q91', p: ['e14'], type: 'ar', lvl: 3, ar: { A: 'Dimensional analysis cannot tell whether K = (1/2)mv² or K = (3/16)mv² is correct.', R: 'Dimensionless constants cannot be obtained by the method of dimensions.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q92', p: ['e14'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The dimensions of m²v³ are:', o: ['[M L² T⁻²]', '[M² L³ T⁻²]', '[M² L² T⁻³]', '[M² L³ T⁻³]'], a: 3, e: 'Not energy.', sol: ['[m²] = [M²]', '[v³] = [L T⁻¹]³ = [L³ T⁻³]', 'Product = [M² L³ T⁻³]', 'Answer: [M² L³ T⁻³] — not [M L² T⁻²], so ruled out'] },
    { id: 'q93', p: ['dr1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'The method of dimensions can deduce a relation when the quantity depends on up to how many physical quantities (linearly independent variables)?', o: ['Two', 'Three', 'Four', 'Any number'], a: 1, e: '1.6.2.' },
    { id: 'q94', p: ['e15'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'If T = k lˣ gʸ mᶻ for a simple pendulum, the values of x, y, z are:', o: ['½, −½, 0', '−½, ½, 0', '½, ½, 1', '1, −1, 0'], a: 0, e: 'Example 1.5.', sol: ['[T] = [L]ˣ [L T⁻²]ʸ [M]ᶻ', 'Equate: L: x + y = 0; T: −2y = 1; M: z = 0', 'y = −½, x = ½, z = 0', 'Answer: T = k√(l/g)'] },
    { id: 'q95', p: ['e15', 'dr2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The method of dimensions gives T = k√(l/g) but cannot give k = 2π.', R: 'Dimensionless constants cannot be obtained by dimensional analysis.' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q96', p: ['e15'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'Using T = 2π√(l/g), the time period of a pendulum of length 1.0 m (g = 9.8 m s⁻²) is about:', o: ['1.0 s', '2.0 s', '3.1 s', '0.5 s'], a: 1, e: 'Example 1.5 formula.', sol: ['Given: l = 1.0 m, g = 9.8 m s⁻²', 'Formula: T = 2π√(l/g)', 'Substitution: 2 × 3.14 × √(1/9.8) = 6.28 × 0.319', 'Answer: ≈ 2.0 s'] },
    { id: 'q97', p: ['dr2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Dimensional analysis distinguishes between physical quantities having same dimensions.', 'Dimensional analysis can test only the dimensional validity of an equation.'], a: 3, e: 'It does not distinguish such quantities.' },
    { id: 'q98', p: ['su1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The SI unit symbol N stands for:', o: ['number', 'nanometre', 'newton', 'neutron'], a: 2, e: 'Summary 6.' },
    { id: 'q99', p: ['su2'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['In computing, units of derived quantities are treated as algebraic quantities.', 'A dimensionally consistent equation must be correct.'], a: 2, e: 'It need not be correct.' },
    { id: 'q100', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'The volume of a cube of side 1 cm in m³ is:', o: ['10⁻³ m³', '10⁻² m³', '10⁻⁶ m³', '10⁻⁹ m³'], a: 2, e: '1 cm = 10⁻² m.', sol: ['Given: side = 1 cm = 10⁻² m', 'Formula: V = a³', 'Substitution: (10⁻²)³', 'Answer: 10⁻⁶ m³'] },
    { id: 'q101', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'A vehicle moving at 18 km h⁻¹ covers in 1 s a distance of:', o: ['18 m', '0.5 m', '50 m', '5 m'], a: 3, e: '1 km h⁻¹ = 5/18 m s⁻¹.', sol: ['Given: v = 18 km h⁻¹', 'Convert: 18 × (5/18) = 5 m s⁻¹', 'Distance in 1 s = 5 × 1', 'Answer: 5 m'] },
    { id: 'q102', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The relative density of lead is 11.3. Its density in kg m⁻³ is:', o: ['1.13 × 10⁴ kg m⁻³', '1.13 × 10³ kg m⁻³', '11.3 kg m⁻³', '1.13 × 10⁵ kg m⁻³'], a: 0, e: '1 g cm⁻³ = 10³ kg m⁻³.', sol: ['Density = 11.3 g cm⁻³', '1 g cm⁻³ = 10³ kg m⁻³', '11.3 × 10³ kg m⁻³', 'Answer: 1.13 × 10⁴ kg m⁻³'] },
    { id: 'q103', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: '1 kg m² s⁻² expressed in g cm² s⁻² is:', o: ['10⁵', '10⁷', '10³', '10⁴'], a: 1, e: '1 J = 10⁷ erg.', sol: ['1 kg = 10³ g, 1 m² = 10⁴ cm²', '1 kg m² s⁻² = 10³ × 10⁴ g cm² s⁻²', '= 10⁷ g cm² s⁻²', 'Answer: 10⁷'] },
    { id: 'q104', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: '3.0 m s⁻² expressed in km h⁻² is:', o: ['1.08 × 10⁴ km h⁻²', '3.9 × 10³ km h⁻²', '3.9 × 10⁴ km h⁻²', '10.8 km h⁻²'], a: 2, e: '1 s = 1/3600 h.', sol: ['3.0 m = 3.0 × 10⁻³ km', '1 s⁻² = (3600)² h⁻² = 1.296 × 10⁷ h⁻²', '3.0 × 10⁻³ × 1.296 × 10⁷ = 3.888 × 10⁴', 'Answer: ≈ 3.9 × 10⁴ km h⁻²'] },
    { id: 'q105', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'G = 6.67 × 10⁻¹¹ N m² kg⁻² expressed in cm³ s⁻² g⁻¹ is:', o: ['6.67 × 10⁻¹¹', '6.67 × 10⁻⁵', '6.67 × 10⁻¹⁴', '6.67 × 10⁻⁸'], a: 3, e: 'N m² kg⁻² = m³ s⁻² kg⁻¹.', sol: ['N m² kg⁻² = kg m s⁻² m² kg⁻² = m³ s⁻² kg⁻¹', 'm³ = 10⁶ cm³; kg⁻¹ = 10⁻³ g⁻¹', 'Factor = 10⁶ × 10⁻³ = 10³', 'Answer: 6.67 × 10⁻⁸ cm³ s⁻² g⁻¹'] },
    { id: 'q106', p: ['xe1'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'In a new system, unit of mass = α kg, length = β m, time = γ s. 1 calorie = 4.2 J has the magnitude:', o: ['4.2 α⁻¹ β⁻² γ²', '4.2 α β² γ⁻²', '4.2 α⁻¹ β² γ⁻²', '4.2 α β⁻² γ²'], a: 0, e: 'n₂ = n₁ (M₁/M₂)(L₁/L₂)²(T₁/T₂)⁻².', sol: ['[Energy] = [M L² T⁻²]', 'n₂ = 4.2 × (1/α)¹ (1/β)² (1/γ)⁻²', '= 4.2 α⁻¹ β⁻² γ²', 'Answer: 4.2 α⁻¹ β⁻² γ²'] },
    { id: 'q107', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A new unit of length is chosen so that the speed of light in vacuum is unity. If light takes 8 min 20 s to reach the earth from the sun, the Sun–Earth distance in the new unit is:', o: ['8.33', '500', '1.5 × 10¹¹', '3 × 10⁸'], a: 1, e: 'Distance = speed × time.', sol: ['Given: c = 1 new unit per second', 't = 8 min 20 s = 500 s', 'Distance = 1 × 500', 'Answer: 500 new units (light-seconds)'] },
    { id: 'q108', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A student sees the width of a hair as 3.5 mm through a microscope of magnification 100. The actual thickness is:', o: ['0.35 mm', '350 mm', '0.035 mm', '3.5 mm'], a: 2, e: 'Actual = observed/magnification.', sol: ['Given: observed = 3.5 mm, m = 100', 'Formula: actual = observed/m', 'Substitution: 3.5/100', 'Answer: 0.035 mm'] },
    { id: 'q109', p: ['xe2'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'A slide picture of area 1.75 cm² is projected on a screen with area 1.55 m². The linear magnification is about:', o: ['8.86 × 10³', '0.89', '886', '94'], a: 3, e: 'Areal magnification = (linear)².', sol: ['Areal magnification = 1.55 × 10⁴ cm² / 1.75 cm² = 8.86 × 10³', 'Linear magnification = √(areal)', '√(8.86 × 10³) ≈ 94.1', 'Answer: ≈ 94'] },
    { id: 'q110', p: ['xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which is the most precise device for measuring length?', o: ['A vernier callipers with 20 divisions on the sliding scale', 'A screw gauge of pitch 1 mm and 100 circular divisions', 'A metre scale', 'An optical instrument measuring within a wavelength of light'], a: 3, e: 'Least count ≈ wavelength of light (~10⁻⁷ m).' },
    { id: 'q111', p: ['xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'A rectangular sheet is 4.234 m × 1.005 m. Its area to correct significant figures is:', o: ['4.255 m²', '4.255170 m²', '4.26 m²', '4.3 m²'], a: 0, e: 'Four significant figures.', sol: ['Given: 4.234 m (4 s.f.), 1.005 m (4 s.f.)', 'Area = 4.234 × 1.005 = 4.25517 m²', 'Keep 4 significant figures', 'Answer: 4.255 m²'] },
    { id: 'q112', p: ['xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'The size of a hydrogen atom is about 0.5 Å. The total atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen atoms is about:', o: ['3 × 10⁻³⁰ m³', '3 × 10⁻⁷ m³', '22.4 × 10⁻³ m³', '6 × 10⁻⁷ m³'], a: 1, e: 'V = NA × (4/3)πr³.', sol: ['r = 0.5 Å = 0.5 × 10⁻¹⁰ m', 'Volume of one atom = (4/3)π(0.5 × 10⁻¹⁰)³ ≈ 5.24 × 10⁻³¹ m³', 'Total = 6.023 × 10²³ × 5.24 × 10⁻³¹ ≈ 3.15 × 10⁻⁷ m³', 'Answer: ≈ 3 × 10⁻⁷ m³'] },
    { id: 'q113', p: ['xe3'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 3, q: 'A boy writes m = m₀/(1 − v²)^½ forgetting c. Using dimensions, the correct relation is:', o: ['m = m₀/(1 − v²/c²)^½', 'm = m₀c/(1 − v²)^½', 'm = m₀/(c² − v²)^½', 'm = m₀/(1 − v/c²)^½'], a: 0, e: '(1 − v²/c²) must be dimensionless.' },
    { id: 'q114', p: ['xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'Mass of the Sun is 2.0 × 10³⁰ kg and radius 7.0 × 10⁸ m. Its average mass density is about:', o: ['1.4 × 10⁶ kg m⁻³', '1.4 kg m⁻³', '1.4 × 10³ kg m⁻³', '1.4 × 10⁻³ kg m⁻³'], a: 2, e: 'Range of solids and liquids, not gases.', sol: ['Formula: ρ = M/((4/3)πR³)', 'Volume = (4/3) × 3.14 × (7.0 × 10⁸)³ ≈ 1.437 × 10²⁷ m³', 'ρ = 2.0 × 10³⁰ / 1.437 × 10²⁷ ≈ 1.39 × 10³', 'Answer: ≈ 1.4 × 10³ kg m⁻³'] },
    { id: 'q115', p: ['xe3'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'Distant hill tops seen from a fast train appear stationary while nearby trees seem to move rapidly backward.', R: 'Distant objects actually move along with the train.' }, a: 2, e: 'R is false; the line of sight to distant objects changes very little.' },
    { id: 'q116', p: ['xe3', 'sr2', 'sr4'], type: 'stmts', lvl: 2, q: 'Which of the following have exactly four significant figures?', s: ['0.2370 g cm⁻³', '6.320 J', '6.032 N m⁻²', '0.007 m²'], o: ['I and II only', 'II, III and IV only', 'I, II and III only', 'I, II, III and IV'], a: 2, e: '0.007 m² has one.' },
    { id: 'q117', p: ['xe3'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'One mole of an ideal gas at STP occupies 22.4 L. Taking the size of a hydrogen molecule as 1 Å, the ratio of molar volume to atomic volume of a mole of hydrogen is about:', o: ['7 × 10⁻⁴', '22.4', '6 × 10²³', '7 × 10⁴'], a: 3, e: 'Gas molecules are far apart.', sol: ['Molecular volume = (4/3)π(0.5 × 10⁻¹⁰)³ × 6.023 × 10²³ ≈ 3.15 × 10⁻⁷ m³', 'Molar volume = 22.4 × 10⁻³ m³', 'Ratio = 22.4 × 10⁻³ / 3.15 × 10⁻⁷', 'Answer: ≈ 7 × 10⁴'] },
    { id: 'q118', p: ['xe2'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'The statement \'atoms are very small objects\' should be reframed as:', o: ['Atoms are very large compared to a cricket ball', 'Atoms are very small compared to a cricket ball', 'Atoms have no size', 'Atoms are small in all comparisons with nuclei'], a: 1, e: 'Large/small needs a standard for comparison.' },
    { id: 'q119', p: ['sr2', 'sf2'], type: 'num', lvl: 1, q: 'Number of significant figures in 0.06900 is:', o: ['4', '6', '2', '3'], a: 0, e: 'Trailing zeros after decimal are significant.', sol: ['Number: 0.06900', 'Leading zeros not significant', 'Significant digits: 6, 9, 0, 0', 'Answer: 4'] },
    { id: 'q120', p: ['si4', 't12'], type: 'num', lvl: 2, q: 'The angle 1° expressed in radian is:', o: ['180/π rad', 'π/180 rad', 'π/360 rad', '1 rad'], a: 1, e: 'Table 1.2.', sol: ['Given: 180° = π rad', 'Formula: 1° = π/180 rad', 'Substitution: 3.1416/180', 'Answer: π/180 ≈ 0.01745 rad'] },
    { id: 'q121', p: ['mol', 't11b'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['One mole contains exactly 6.02214076 × 10²³ elementary entities.', 'An elementary entity may be a specified group of particles.'], a: 0, e: 'Both true.' },
    { id: 'q122', p: ['ar2'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'One light year computed from c = 3.00 × 10⁸ m s⁻¹ and 1 y = 3.1557 × 10⁷ s is written as 9.47 × 10¹⁵ m.', R: 'In multiplication the result keeps the least number of significant figures (three here).' }, a: 0, e: 'R explains A.' },
    { id: 'q123', p: ['e12'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['The measured mass 5.74 g has only 2 significant figures.', 'In Example 1.2 the density is reported to 2 significant figures.'], a: 3, e: 'Mass has 3; volume 1.2 cm³ has 2.' },
    { id: 'q124', p: ['di3', 'df1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Which of the following has dimensions different from the others?', o: ['Speed', 'Average velocity', 'Acceleration', 'Change in velocity'], a: 2, e: 'Acceleration is [L T⁻²]; others are [L T⁻¹].' },
    { id: 'q125', p: ['da1'], type: 'st', lvl: 2, st: ['Dimensional analysis helps in checking the dimensional consistency of equations.', 'Units cannot be treated like algebraic symbols.'], a: 2, e: 'Units and dimensions are treated as algebraic symbols.' },
    { id: 'q126', p: ['dc3', 'dc1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 2, q: 'Which equation is dimensionally incorrect?', o: ['x = x₀ + v₀t + ½at²', 'v = v₀ + at', 'v² = v₀² + 2ax', 'x = x₀ + v₀t² + ½at²'], a: 3, e: 'v₀t² has dimensions [L T].' },
    { id: 'q127', p: ['e13'], type: 'ar', lvl: 2, ar: { A: 'The equation ½mv² = mgh is dimensionally incorrect.', R: 'Both sides have dimensions [M L² T⁻²].' }, a: 3, e: 'A is false; both sides have the same dimensions.' },
    { id: 'q128', p: ['dr1', 'e15'], type: 'num', lvl: 3, q: 'The speed v of a wave on a string depends on tension F ([M L T⁻²]) and mass per unit length μ ([M L⁻¹]). By dimensional analysis, v ∝:', o: ['F/μ', '√(μ/F)', '√(F/μ)', 'Fμ'], a: 2, e: 'Product-type dependence.', sol: ['Let v = k Fᵃ μᵇ', '[L T⁻¹] = [M L T⁻²]ᵃ [M L⁻¹]ᵇ → M: a + b = 0; L: a − b = 1; T: −2a = −1', 'a = ½, b = −½', 'Answer: v ∝ √(F/μ)'] },
    { id: 'q129', p: ['su2', 'sn1'], type: 'mcq', lvl: 1, q: 'Very small and very large quantities are conveniently expressed using:', o: ['Scientific notation with powers of 10 and prefixes', 'Only significant figures', 'Only dimensional formulae', 'Roman numerals'], a: 0, e: 'Summary 7.' }
  ]
});
